# AN ARCH LINUX MICROVM RUNNING i3 ON AN X SERVER IN MEMORY.
#
# THE SAME SHAPE AS THE SWAY VARIANT, WITH ONE STRUCTURAL DIFFERENCE: THIS IS X11, SO THE
# DISPLAY SERVER IS `Xvfb` AND THE CAPTURE PATH IS XSHM RATHER THAN wlr-screencopy.
#
# WHY AN X SERVER AT ALL, AND WHY THAT ONE. A microvm has no GPU and, without vkms, no DRM
# node; Xorg's `modesetting` driver has nothing to open. `Xvfb` needs neither - it renders
# into its own process memory - which is why the whole X11 family works on a machine that
# cannot run Xorg. MEASURED: i3 draws its bar along the bottom with a clock and the network address.
#
# EVERY X11 DESKTOP NEEDS A SESSION BUS, and this image gives it one: `dbus-run-session`
# wraps the session manager. MEASURED on XFCE before that line existed, the desktop started
# and drew "Unable to contact settings server / Could not connect", which is what a desktop
# without a bus looks like.
#
# I3 ALSO SHOWS A FIRST-RUN CONFIGURATION DIALOG, so the assertion names the window manager
# rather than the dialog: the dialog is a one-time thing and the bar is i3 working.

walkthrough "i3 microvm - i3 on xvfb, captured over xshm"
  resolution 1024x768
  recording_resolution 1024x768

  needs arch-vmlinuz arch-initramfs arch.qcow2
  disk arch.qcow2

  boot as a microvm from kernel "arch-vmlinuz" and initramfs "arch-initramfs" with "console=ttyS0 root=/dev/vda rw rootfstype=ext4" without a display with memory 1536, waiting up to 240s

  # THE CONSOLE REACHES A LOGIN, which is the boot working.
  the console will say "login"

  # AND THE SESSION IS vmuser's - PipeWire refuses root (`ConditionUser=!root`) and every
  # desktop here goes through it for audio.
  the console will say "vmuser"

  # THE BAR, WHICH ONLY RUNS INSIDE i3.
  #
  # MEASURED ON RUN 44925759474: the console's last lines were
  #
  #	desktop.sh[285]: i3status: trying to auto-detect output_format setting
  #	desktop.sh[285]: i3status: auto-detected "i3bar"
  #
  # WHICH IS i3 WORKING. The old assertion asked for the literal "i3-wm" - that is a PACKAGE NAME,
  # not something the window manager prints, so the check could not pass on a machine that was
  # drawing perfectly, and this variant spent its three minutes failing on a healthy desktop.
  #
  # i3status is i3's own bar process: it is started by i3's config, it writes this line while it
  # decides what to draw, and it says "i3bar" only when it has a bar to draw into.
  the console will say "i3status"
  the console will say "i3bar"

  wait 20s

  stop the machine by killing it, waiting up to 60s
